<BaseLayout
  title="How this directory is built"
  description="The sourcing rules behind longislandoysterfarm.com: what we publish, what we refuse to publish, and how every fact is cited and dated."
>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="text-shell-600 text-sm">
    <a href="/">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> <span>Methodology</span>
  </nav>

  <h1 class="font-display mt-2 text-4xl">How this directory is built</h1>

  <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-lg">
    A directory is only worth reading if you can tell where it got its facts. Every listing here
    cites its sources and records the date a person last checked them.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl">What we publish</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose">Every published fact traces to one of four kinds of source:</p>
  <ol class="mt-3 max-w-prose list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
    <li>
      <strong>The business’s own published material</strong> — its site, its own social accounts,
      its own price list. The best source for what a farm sells and what it calls its oysters.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Government records</strong> — the Suffolk County Shellfish Aquaculture Lease Program
      inventory and its public GIS services, New York State DEC material, and town shellfish and
      mariculture programs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Institutional programs</strong> — Cornell Cooperative Extension of Suffolk County and
      New York Sea Grant.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Named press and non-profit coverage</strong>, for context only — never as the only
      source for a contact detail, an address, or a claim about how a farm operates.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl">What we refuse to publish</h2>
  <ul class="mt-3 max-w-prose list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
    <li>
      <strong>A business inferred from a lease.</strong> A lease is a property record. Many
      leaseholders are private individuals with no public business, and some businesses hold no
      lease at all.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Personal names.</strong> A grower’s name appears only where the business itself
      publishes it. Lease records name individuals; we do not republish those names.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Private addresses.</strong> Only an address a business publishes as a place of
      business — never a residence, even where a public record connects one to the operation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Map pins we cannot stand behind.</strong> Every coordinate carries a precision. A
      lease centroid is a point of open water, not somewhere you can visit, and is never used as a
      business location.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Copied descriptions.</strong> A farm’s own words belong to the farm.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Plausible guesses.</strong> If a source does not say it, the field stays empty and the
      page says “Not listed”. That is not the same as “no”.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl">What this directory does not yet cover</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose">
    The county lease program covers the Peconic and Gardiners bay systems only. South Shore growers
    — the Great South Bay, Moriches and Shinnecock — work bay bottom leased from their
    <em>town</em>, and hatcheries and nurseries hold no bay lease at all. None of them appear in the
    county dataset, so our coverage of them is thinner and is being built from town programs and the
    farms’ own publications. A farm’s absence from this directory is not evidence it does not exist.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl">Staying current</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose">
    Each listing shows when it was last verified, and anything older than about six months is
    flagged for re-checking. Contact details carry a stricter rule: they are removed rather than
    kept if we cannot re-confirm them from a source read within the last year. Stale contact details
    are the fastest way to send someone to a farm that has closed.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl">The same rule applies to the guides</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose">
    Our <a href="/guides/">guides</a> are original writing, but prose is where a sourcing rule is
    easiest to break: the moment a guide names a temperature, a month, a regulation or a health
    risk, it is making a factual claim. So guides carry the same burden a listing does — a citation
    list and a date the facts were last checked — and a guide that gives food-safety information
    cannot be published unless it cites a public-health authority by name. The build refuses it
    otherwise.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose">
    Each guide also publishes the questions it <em>could not</em> answer. Six guides were planned
    for this section and two were published; the other four are held back because we could not
    source them, and what each one is missing is written down rather than left to memory. An
    unsourced guide is worth less than no guide.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl">Corrections</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose">
    Any farm can <a href="/claim/">correct or remove its listing</a>. Removal requests are honoured
    without argument and without proof of ownership. Nothing on this site publishes automatically; a
    person reviews every change.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl">Independence</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose">
    No farm pays to be listed, and no listing can be bought, improved or removed with money. Where
    the shop earns a commission on a product, that is disclosed on the page it appears on.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
